15.2 Chatbot accuracy testing
Create a test set of at least 30 questions.

Include:
- Questions that closely match stored FAQs.
- Questions with different wording.
- Questions containing spelling mistakes.
- Ambiguous questions.
- Questions unrelated to college information.
Record the expected answer or fallback for each test.

Calculate answer-selection accuracy for questions with known expected FAQs:
\[
\text{Accuracy} =
\frac{\text{Correct FAQ selections}}
{\text{Total test questions with expected FAQs}}
\times 100
\]
Also track:
- Correct fallback rate for out-of-scope questions.
- Incorrect answer rate.
- Response time.
- Student feedback.
Don't report a made-up accuracy figure. Calculate it from your actual test results.

For a rule-based system, matching accuracy can vary substantially with the question wording and the quality of the FAQ keywords.
15.3 Security testing
Test that:
- Unauthenticated requests cannot modify FAQs.
- User messages cannot execute SQL.
- Invalid JSON does not crash the application.
- User input is rendered as text rather than executable HTML.
- Secrets are not included in Git commits.
- Feedback and chat endpoints validate their inputs.
- The application does not expose detailed stack traces to users.
