17. Optional Advanced Features
Only consider these after the basic chatbot, database, and admin panel work correctly.
17.1 TF-IDF and cosine similarity
Use scikit-learn to represent questions numerically and compare their similarity.

This can improve matching when questions use different wording.

Useful concepts to learn:
- Text preprocessing
- TF-IDF vectorization
- Cosine similarity
- Similarity thresholds
17.2 AI-powered responses
An LLM API can make the chatbot more flexible when students ask questions that do not closely resemble stored FAQs.

However, it introduces:
- API costs or usage limits.
- Network dependency.
- Additional configuration.
- Potentially incorrect answers.
For a college assistant, retrieve relevant official information first and instruct the model to answer only from that information. If the necessary information is missing, it should say so rather than invent an answer.

Never expose an API key in frontend JavaScript or commit it to GitHub.
17.3 Document-based question answering
Allow administrators to upload approved college documents, such as:
- Student handbooks.
- Admission guidelines.
- Examination regulations.
- Fee circulars.
The application could extract relevant passages and use them to answer student questions.

This feature requires additional work around document parsing, indexing, access control, and keeping information up to date.
17.4 Multilingual support
Allow students to ask questions in English and other languages supported by your chosen matching or language model.

This can improve accessibility, but it should be tested with real questions in each supported language.
17.5 Analytics dashboard
Show:
- Most frequently asked questions.
- Questions that received fallback responses.
- FAQs with negative feedback.
- Number of interactions per day.
Use aggregated statistics and avoid collecting unnecessary personal information.
